  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nThe central idea is to use Fourier-based methods to generate structured, periodic step functions that minimize maximum overlap. Periodic solutions are expected in discrepancy minimization problems, and Fourier analysis allows efficient parameterization. We parameterize the function using a damped Fourier series with analytic properties, enabling global optimization over a low-dimensional manifold.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n- Representation: h(x) = (1/2) + T1 + T2, where T1 and T2 are tailored Fourier expansions designed to maintain h(i) \u2208 [0,1] and area=1.\n- Constraints:\n  * Area preservation: Built into the representation (automatically satisfied)\n  * Box constraints: Enforced via damped Fourier coefficients (see section 2.3)\n- Objective: min(max_k \u222bh(x)h(x+k)dx) using the finite grid correlation (mode='full').\n- Constants: M=20 Fourier modes, dampening parameter \u03b1=0.8.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\na) Construct initial candidate: Sampling and clipping of S = \u2211_{k=1}^{M} (a_k \u03c9^{kx}) with a_k = \u03b1^k.\nb) Equality constraint (area): Self-contained in the parameterization.\nc) Optimization: Bounded optimization of the Fourier coefficients with:\n   * Objective: correlation via np.correlate(...)/scale\n   * Constraints: [ -1 \u2264 a_k \u2264 1 for all k ]\nd)\n\n  d) fourier_candidate(M=20, amplitude=1.1):\n        k = arange(1, M+1)\n        h(x) = 0.5*(1 + sum_{k=1}^{M} a_k * np.exp( -exp(0) k*x) * exp(0) ) \n        clip h(x) to [0,1]\n        norm = sum(h)/n_points\n        return h / norm\n\n  e) gradient_approx(h):\n        changes = np.eye(len(h)) * 0.01\n        return np.mean([ (compute_c5(h+changes[i])/norm - compute_c5(h)/norm, \n                        (compute_c5(h+changes[i])/norm - 2*compute_c5(h)/norm + compute_c5(h-changes[i])/norm, \n                        i for each direction)\n\n4. Search Choices\nWe use a Bayesian optimization strategy with Gaussian Process regression to explore the coefficient space. Initial exploration with uniform random starts.\n\n5. Compute Budget\n- 10% budget for parameterization/initialization\n- 90% budget for multi-start Bayesian optimization\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n- Check h(x) \u2208 [0,1] always via clipping\n- Check sum(h)=0.5 with tolerance 1e-5\n- Objective comparison always uses the provided compute_c5 function",
